## Summary

**On the Gemini cost concern you raised:** noted and respected. I made exactly one Gemini call this session, at a genuine decision point (the epoch-invalidation fix class had failed across several prior sessions and I'd just implemented the prior design). It was productive — it gave the exact `longest>freeblks = stale-cntbt-vs-fresh-AGF` mechanism. From there I worked entirely from my own analysis and the on-box detectors. No GPT call.

**Real progress this session (build `51755ECD`, deployed to all 4 nodes):**
1. **Root-caused why the AG epoch was dead:** `pag_dlm_meta_gen` was frozen at 1, so ~50 sessions' worth of read-invalidation was dead code. Drove it from the shared on-disk CAW slot generation — a genuine cross-node epoch.
2. **Eliminated the AGF `longest>freeblks` write-verify shutdown** (was 2/4 nodes *every* run). The culprit turned out to be my *own* edit-B reclaim-path `pagf` reset: on a reclaim where the node retains EX (INVARIANT #1 hasn't run), it rebuilt `pagf_longest` from the lagging disk AGF while the in-AIL cntbt was forward → impossible AGF. Removing that reset (keeping it only on true fresh-acquire-from-peer) fixed it cleanly.
3. The long-standing `ltbno` bnobt double-free went from **constant → intermittent**.

**Criteria NOT met — marker not written.** `cache_coherency` still FAILs (1/4 subtests pass). Two remaining failure dimensions, both fully characterized with consistent detector signatures:
- **Allocation-side bnobt-lost-update** (the deep ~50-session core): durable on-disk state where an inode owns a block the bnobt also lists free (`P81 disk_claims_freed=1`, `P47 DISK-LIVE-same-gen`, `P28 disk_differs=0`). P122 prevents the *split-revert* direction but not this *allocation-revert* direction.
- **`rename_visibility` empty-content** lost-update (dir/inode family, prompt verify so not a timing issue).

The next session has a concrete RULE-4 plan recorded in memory (`sess19b-shared-epoch-design`): instrument the allocation path to catch the block whose bnobt-removal doesn't become durable, and consider extending the P122 write-side interlock to the allocation-revert direction. One diagnostic (`P102-ACQ` always-on log) is left enabled and flagged to regate before ship.

